---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../../data/standards/ifric/ifric10.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType="IFRS"
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Definitions" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Core Question" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The No-Reversal Rule for Goodwill" },
        { id: "s5", label: "The No-Reversal Rule for Equity Instruments" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Worked Example – Quarterly Impairment" },
        { id: "s7", label: "What IFRS 9 Changed in Practice" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Assets Where Reversal IS Permitted" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "IFRIC 10 vs US GAAP" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">A collision between two defensible principles</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 34 treats each interim period as an integral part of the annual period, generally favouring the SAME recognition and measurement principles used at year end. But IAS 36 permanently prohibits reversing a goodwill impairment loss once recognised. IFRIC 10 exists because, without guidance, an entity could argue an interim impairment "washes out" by year end if conditions recover – a result IAS 36's own annual-period logic was designed to prevent.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Consistency between interim and annual periods</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRIC 10's conclusion ensures an impairment loss recognised in ANY interim period within a financial year is treated exactly as it would be if that same loss had been recognised at the year end – consistent, comparable, and immune to which specific quarter the underlying trigger happened to be identified in.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope & Definitions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IFRIC 10 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRIC 10 addresses the interaction between IAS 34 and the impairment requirements of IAS 36 for goodwill, and (historically) IAS 39 for investments in equity instruments and financial assets carried at cost. It applies to any entity that reports both interim AND annual financial statements applying IFRS.</div>
        <div class="elabel">ASSETS IN SCOPE OF THE NO-REVERSAL RULE</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Goodwill</span>
          <span>Investments in equity instruments</span>
          <span>Financial assets carried at cost because their fair value cannot be reliably measured</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Interim period</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A financial reporting period shorter than a full financial year, per IAS 34 – typically a quarter or half-year.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Impairment loss</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The amount by which the carrying amount of an asset or cash-generating unit exceeds its recoverable amount, per IAS 36.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Core Question</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">What IFRIC 10 actually decides</div>
        <div class="principle">If an entity recognises an impairment loss on goodwill or a relevant equity or cost-measured financial asset in an interim period, and the underlying indicators that triggered the loss REVERSE by the next reporting date, may the entity reverse the previously recognised impairment loss? IFRIC 10's answer is a firm NO.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The No-Reversal Rule for Goodwill</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Interim impairment losses on goodwill are permanent</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">An entity shall NOT reverse an impairment loss recognised in a previous interim period in respect of goodwill, even if no loss, or a smaller loss, would have been recognised had the impairment assessment been performed only at the end of a subsequent reporting period. This directly extends IAS 36's absolute prohibition on reversing goodwill impairment into the interim-reporting context.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>The No-Reversal Rule for Equity Instruments</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Historically extended to certain financial assets too</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">The same no-reversal logic historically applied to impairment losses on investments in equity instruments, and on financial assets carried at cost because fair value could not be reliably measured, under the former IAS 39 impairment model – such losses, once recognised through profit or loss in an interim period, could not be reversed even if the equity instrument's value later recovered.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example – Quarterly Impairment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Goodwill impairment recognised in Q2, recovering by Q4</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example: No reversal permitted despite recovery</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A cash-generating unit carries goodwill of $3,000,000. At the end of Q2, a recoverable amount assessment identifies an impairment loss of $500,000, which is recognised in the Q2 interim financial statements, reducing goodwill to $2,500,000. By Q4, the same cash-generating unit's recoverable amount has recovered such that no impairment would have been indicated had the assessment first been performed at that date.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">The $500,000 impairment loss recognised in Q2 CANNOT be reversed in Q4 or at year end. Goodwill remains carried at $2,500,000 in the annual financial statements, notwithstanding the year-end recoverable amount assessment showing no impairment on a stand-alone basis.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>What IFRS 9 Changed in Practice</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">The equity-instrument leg is now largely academic</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Under IFRS 9, equity instruments are generally measured at fair value, with changes recognised either in profit or loss, or – if an irrevocable FVOCI election is made – in other comprehensive income, with NO subsequent recycling to profit or loss even on disposal. Since IFRS 9 does not use an impairment model for equity instruments in the way IAS 39 did, IFRIC 10's equity-instrument guidance has little remaining practical application; its GOODWILL guidance, however, remains fully operative and is the interpretation's primary ongoing relevance today.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Assets Where Reversal IS Permitted</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">A narrow, specific prohibition – not a blanket rule</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">IFRIC 10's no-reversal conclusion is specific to goodwill (always) and to the historical IAS 39 equity or cost-measured asset categories. It does NOT prevent reversal of impairment losses on OTHER assets within IAS 36's scope – such as property, plant and equipment or intangible assets other than goodwill – which remain eligible for impairment reversal in a LATER period if the recoverable amount genuinely recovers.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">What IFRIC 10 layers onto IAS 34/IAS 36</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Nature and amount of any impairment loss recognised or reversed during an interim period</span>
          <span>Explanation where an interim impairment loss on goodwill has not been reversed despite an apparent later recovery in indicators</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments & Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Whether impairment indicators genuinely exist at an interim date</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Recoverable amount of the cash-generating unit at the interim date</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IFRIC 10 vs US GAAP</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – IFRIC 10 vs US GAAP (ASC 270 / ASC 350)</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>IFRS (IFRIC 10)</span><span>US GAAP</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Reversal of goodwill impairment generally</span><span>Always prohibited, at both interim and annual level</span><span>Also always prohibited under ASC 350</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Explicit interim-specific interpretation</span><span>Yes – IFRIC 10 directly addresses the interim or annual interaction</span><span>ASC 270 embeds a similar "integral part of the annual period" philosophy</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">On goodwill specifically, the two frameworks reach a highly converged outcome; IFRIC 10's distinct contribution is making explicit that the SAME absolute rule applies even to a loss recognised only in an interim period.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Where every IFRIC 10 event lands</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Interim impairment loss on goodwill</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Recognised in the interim period; carried forward permanently</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Apparent recovery of recoverable amount by year end</span><span>No entry</span><span>No reversal recognised – the prior loss stands</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Impairment loss on non-goodwill PPE or intangibles reversing later</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Reversal permitted under general IAS 36 rules</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IAS 34</span><span>Supplies the interim-reporting framework and the general principle that interim periods follow annual recognition and measurement principles</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>IAS 36</span><span>Supplies the absolute no-reversal rule for goodwill that IFRIC 10 extends explicitly to interim periods</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IFRS 9</span><span>Superseded IAS 39's equity-instrument impairment model</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>IFRS 3</span><span>Governs the initial recognition of goodwill that IFRIC 10's no-reversal principle protects from write-back</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Cash-generating unit (CGU)</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The smallest identifiable group of assets that generates cash inflows largely independent of other assets, the level at which goodwill impairment is tested under IAS 36.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Recoverable amount</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The higher of an asset's (or CGU's) fair value less costs of disposal and its value in use – compared against carrying amount to determine impairment under IAS 36.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Cash-generating unit (CGU)", definition: "The smallest identifiable group of assets that generates cash inflows largely independent of other assets, the level at which goodwill impairment is tested under IAS 36." },
      { term: "Recoverable amount", definition: "The higher of an asset's (or CGU's) fair value less costs of disposal and its value in use." },
      { term: "No-reversal principle", definition: "The IAS 36 rule, extended by IFRIC 10 to interim periods, that once a goodwill impairment loss is recognised it can never be written back." }
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.IFRIC10} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>




